good afternoon we at the American Stock  Exchange together with Donaldson Lufkin  and generate Merrill Lynch and Smith  Barney are proud to be sharing this  great adventure with you I think I think  we are demonstrating that our nation's  can cooperate to their mutual advantage  every year before these sessions get  underway one of the first questions that  I'm asked by overseas visitors is what  is the thinking of the federal reserve  board this year I was able to respond  that we would soon hear the board's  viewpoint firsthand from its  vice-chairman manual Johnson when dr.  Johnson first became a board member in  February of 1986 some thought that his  strong economic views would rile the  waters during difficult decision making  but he quickly showed that he would  avoid personal feuds and focuses  considerable intellect on advancing  furthering the mission of the Federal  Reserve that's the same kind of  professionalism that dr. Johnson is  demonstrated in his earlier performance  at the Treasury Department there he won  the department's highest honor award  known as the Alexander Hamilton Award  while at the Treasury as assistant  secretary he had a major role in  formulating the United States economic  policy dr. Johnson holds a doctorate in  economics he's a university professor of  economics and he's written four books in  over 50 articles on political economy  and public policy certainly the time  could not be more appropriate nor the  man more appropriate than dr. Manuel  Johnson  thank you very much our ID it's my  pleasure to be here today I maybe might  I realize from your perspective the  timing is appropriate I'm not sure from  my viewpoint picked the perfect time to  be here today but but I but I think in a  way I I really have maybe this is the  most convenient time for me to address  this group at a time when there seems to  be some volatility in the stock markets  and some concern because I think it's  important to try and point out some of  the economic fundamentals that exist in  the free trading system in the US  economy in particular today and and try  and see if we can't focus on those  issues and avoid some of the current  uneasiness with animal spirits and the  markets and I think that's a good place  to start I think really in terms of  talking about the economy we should we  should briefly look at the situation in  some historical perspective before  talking about the implications of  current issues and I think the the first  thing that comes to mind when you look  at the performance of the US economy  over the last five years is that that  the economy indeed has expanded under  interrupted for five years about to  enter its six-year of economic expansion  and that this is now the longest  peacetime expansion in the post-war era  and I think you can go back good deal  further than that in addition to that  and I think the more the other very  interesting fact about current economic  issues is that that not only is this the  longest peacetime expansion but even in  spite of what's been happening recently  inflation rate and interest rates are as  low or lower today than they were when  this recovery began at the  nineteen eighty two most of you may find  that unusual but that's still still a  fact that interest rates and inflation  rates are generally is lower lower today  than when this expansion began and that  is also a very unusual event that's  really unprecedented in post-war  expansions now I don't want to sit here  and say though that that the that the  economic situation doesn't have its risk  I think it's important to point out some  of these fundamental strengths and I  think that this does point out that the  future course of the economy has great  potential that given these fundamental  facts that the inflationary pressures  seem to be modest and that interest  rates even though they have gone up  recently or still lower than when the  expansion began indicates that there is  still quite a bit of strength  fundamental strength to the economy and  that that the free trading system has  the opportunity to continue expanding  for some time without destabilizing  forces taking place now as I said this  situation is not without its risk of  course and I think that there are some  obvious issues that you're all well  aware of that have to be addressed and  ultimately dealt with if we're going to  continue to have an economic expansion  without financial instability and  inflationary problems and those obvious  issues are associated primarily with the  US federal budget deficit and our  deficit they in trade and the associated  surpluses in the industrial among the  industrial trading partners so indeed  there are these international imbalances  of large trade deficit in the United  States and large peoplee large trade  surpluses among its industrial trading  partners and a very large federal budget  deficit still to be addressed  but I should point out though that  progress is even being made there I  think we shouldn't lose sight of some of  the progress that's been made in this  particular area I think one of the  concerns of course about about such  large imbalances in the pre trading  system is though that with the u.s.  running a significant federal budget  deficit and requiring a substantial  amount of private savings for its  financing as well as trying to continue  to have an investment expansion at the  same time requires a continuation of net  capital inflows from abroad because our  domestic savings is not at a level to  satisfy both domestic investment and  government consumption and therefore  there is a need for capital in fort  imports from abroad and as long as  that's the case there has to be the  other side of the coin which is a  current account deficit and so as long  as we are reliant on foreign sources of  capital flows to sustain the domestic  economy as well as government  consumption there will be a persistent  trade deficit and a growing external  debt but as I mentioned a minute ago I  think that that's the major concern but  as I mentioned a minute ago I think that  we have to focus on some of the games  that have been made in this area and  realize that we have made a good deal of  progress and that we roughly have the  these imbalances moving in the right  direction and the economy is generally  on the right track let me point out that  that just during this last fiscal year  at the US federal budget deficit has  come down between 60 and 70 billion  dollars from its peak level of 221  billion dollars in 19th and fiscal 1986  this is a dramatic turnaround in the  federal budget deficit equal to about  1.5 to 1.6 percent of gross national  product which is an exceptional a change  in the direction of  a federal spending as a percentage GNP  and as an absolute amount and this has  taken place with no basic improvement in  the nominal current account deficit at  this point in other words we're still  receiving into the u.s. basically the  same net capital inflow as before and on  top of that we've had roughly a 60 to 70  billion dollar reduction in the federal  budget deficit in other words we've had  a 60 to 70 billion dollar reduction in  government demands own own private  saving both domestically and from abroad  so that there has been an increased  capacity for private domestic investment  through the sum of the relief in the  direction of the federal budget deficit  and I think that this needs to be  pointed out in addition to that the  Congress passed and the president signed  an additional amended version of the  gramm-rudman-hollings amendment and this  will require an additional twenty three  billion dollars in in either budget  reduction or spending reduction  specifically depending on how the budget  priorities get resolved in this Congress  but in fact they did the Congress and  the president did renew the sequester  ation process by making a constitutional  through allowing