ficit and I think one of the  reasons for this is that it takes awhile  for contracts to be renegotiated at the  changed prices so that in fact you get  larger volumes under old contract prices  which temporarily intensifies the  nominal trade account before it gets  better of course this is known as the  j-curve effect where first you see an  improvement in real terms in the volume  of trade before you see an improvement  in the actual amount that you have to  finance which is the  aminul current account but of course we  have seen the current account peek out  over recent months and not increase in  size in addition to that I think if you  were to subtract that the data show that  if you were subtract out all imports  that you would find that the nominal  current account has actually been  improving to in the areas of a non oil  trade in fact the bilateral trade  balance has been showing some  improvement between the two major circle  surplus countries between Germany and  Japan so part of the problem is that the  recent rise in oil prices back from the  ten to the twenty dollar range has  masked what would have been an  improvement by now in the nominal  current account deficit and I think this  shouldn't go unrecognized because of  this I think temporary effect of the oil  price on own oil import values you  haven't seen this improvement but in  fact the Federal Reserve is forecasting  a fairly significant improvement  phenomenal current account for 1988 and  a continued improvement in real terms on  the order of what we've already seen so  I think that even in terms of the trade  imbalance situation we are moving  generally in the right directions these  imbalances are coming down both in the  domestic budget and in the external debt  situation and so the stage i think is  being set for a healthy continued  expansion without without inflationary  instability however there are still  other questions and i think because of  these imbalances about the future course  of economic policy in the US and maybe  this is one of the sources of financial  uncertainty that permeates the market  today and has led to some of the the  Troublesome volatility that we've seen i  think a significant question in the  financial markets is actually how policy  is going to deal with these imbalances  in the future and what direction  economic policy will in fact take and  will the direction of policy in  terms of fiscal and regulatory policy  put the Federal Reserve in a tight box  that makes our decisions more difficult  and and has to make tough decisions  about the inflationary instability that  would otherwise not have to make and I  think that's some of the concern that  maybe in the financial markets today and  so it matters how we continue to try and  resolve these imbalances and I think  that one the things that we don't need  to see in the future our quick fix  solutions to the trade imbalance for  instance are for that matter in the  federal budget deficit the last thing we  need is in today's environment is is a  quick fix approach to the federal trade  deficit by say trying to already  artificially depress the dollar severely  below current levels this would simply I  think exacerbate the financial market  conditions and lead to further potential  for financial problems and in fact I  think that we already have a  depreciation in the dollar that is  underlying the improvement we're seeing  in the trade deficit at this point the  dollar has declined from its peak levels  in early nineteen eighty-five by about  forty to fifty percent against the major  industrial countries currencies the  Federal Reserve we're not hearing any  industry representatives over the 12  districts of the Federal Reserve that  are saying today that they can't compete  at roughly this level of exchange rates  in fact we're seeing strong pick up  strong improvement in orders for export  and for import substitution type  products so in fact we are getting the  rec